# ── Waitlist → Google Sheets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used only by the serverless function api/waitlist.js. Both are server-side:
# set them in the Vercel dashboard (Project → Settings → Environment Variables),
# never with a VITE_ prefix — that would ship the private key to the browser.
#
# There are no client-side env vars. The landing page posts to /api/waitlist
# and needs no configuration of its own.

# The FULL service account JSON key, as a single line.
# Create it in Google Cloud Console → IAM → Service Accounts → Keys → Add key
# (JSON), then paste the whole file contents as the value. Keep the embedded
# \n escapes inside private_key exactly as Google emits them — they are part of
# the JSON string and JSON.parse restores the real newlines.
GOOGLE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON={"type":"service_account","project_id":"...","private_key_id":"...","private_key":"-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n","client_email":"divyakarya@your-project.iam.gserviceaccount.com","client_id":"...","token_uri":"https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token"}

# The spreadsheet ID, from its URL:
#   https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/<SHEET_ID>/edit
GOOGLE_SHEET_ID=your_spreadsheet_id_here

# Two things are easy to miss when setting this up:
#   1. Share the spreadsheet with the service account's client_email address,
#      with Editor access. Without it every write fails with a 403.
#   2. The sheet needs a tab named exactly "Waitlist". The function writes
#      columns A–D: email, interest, ISO timestamp, IST timestamp — and reads
#      column A to reject duplicates.
